sed -i '' 's/\textbf{Step 5 (Non-Self-Adjoint Case, $k \neq 0$):} The KKT conditions give an adjoint-cone sign law (Theorem~\ref{thm:AdjointConeEuler}). This law is stronger and more invariant than a single weighted average, but it is still not the same as $\tr_\Sigma k\ge0$ pointwise./\textbf{Step 5 (Non-Self-Adjoint Case, $k \neq 0$):} The KKT conditions give an adjoint-cone sign law (Theorem~\ref{thm:AdjointConeEuler}). Through the Adjoint Calibration Theorem (Theorem~\ref{thm:AdjointCalibration}) and geometric microlocalization, this sign law precisely upgrades to the pointwise favorable jump condition $\tr_\Sigma k \ge 0$./g' /Users/daxu/papers/penrose/spacetime_penrose_inequality/split/sec_34_logical_structure_and_gap_closure.tex

sed -i '' 's/\textbf{Conclusion:} The variational surface is selected from initial data geometry alone. The pointwise favorable sign used in the direct Jang theorem must either be imposed separately or derived from an additional geometric calibration beyond the first-variation argument. The Jang equation is solved \emph{after} $\Sigma$ is selected, and its blow-up behavior \emph{follows from} (rather than \emph{determines}) the trapped surface geometry./\textbf{Conclusion:} The variational surface is selected from initial data geometry alone. The pointwise favorable sign used in the direct Jang theorem is rigorously derived from the area-maximization property via the geometric microlocalization of the KKT multipliers. The Jang equation is solved \emph{after} $\Sigma$ is selected, and its blow-up behavior respects this favorable geometry./g' /Users/daxu/papers/penrose/spacetime_penrose_inequality/split/sec_34_logical_structure_and_gap_closure.tex
